### Why do we need memory barriers?

Modern CPUs reorder instructions and buffer writes in per-core store buffers for speed. This reordering is invisible to a single thread (the single-thread illusion is maintained), but it is visible to other threads, which can see writes in a surprising order. Memory barriers constrain this reordering.

### What are the four barrier types?

| Barrier | Prevents | Banking Example |
|---------|----------|------------------|
| LoadLoad | Later read moving before earlier read | Reading account status before reading the balance that was updated with the status |
| StoreStore | Later write moving before earlier write | Writing the "done" flag before the balance is flushed to memory |
| LoadStore | Later write moving before earlier read | Writing a derived value before reading its input |
| StoreLoad | Later read moving before earlier write | The most expensive barrier; drains the store buffer so all writes become globally visible before any subsequent read |

### What are acquire and release semantics?

Release semantics guarantee that everything written before a release-store is visible to any thread that performs an acquire-load of the same variable. Acquire semantics guarantee that once you read the flag, all subsequent reads see the published data.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.VarHandle;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AcquireReleaseDemo {

    private BigDecimal amount;
    private boolean ready;

    private static final VarHandle READY;
    static {
        try {
            READY = MethodHandles.lookup()
                .findVarHandle(AcquireReleaseDemo.class, "ready", boolean.class);
        } catch (ReflectiveOperationException e) {
            throw new ExceptionInInitializerError(e);
        }
    }

    // Publisher thread
    public void publish() {
        amount = new BigDecimal("50000");         // ordinary write
        VarHandle.releaseFence();                 // StoreStore + LoadStore
        READY.setVolatile(this, true);            // flag write with release semantics
    }

    // Consumer thread
    public void consume() {
        if ((boolean) READY.getVolatile(this)) {  // acquire read of flag
            VarHandle.acquireFence();             // LoadLoad + LoadStore
            processPayment(amount);               // guaranteed to see 50000
        }
    }
}
```

### How does Java expose fences via VarHandle?

The VarHandle class provides five explicit fence methods. These are low-level tools for lock-free data structure authors.

| Method | Barrier Combination | Cost |
|--------|--------------------|----- |
| `VarHandle.fullFence()` | All four barriers | Highest |
| `VarHandle.acquireFence()` | LoadLoad + LoadStore | Medium |
| `VarHandle.releaseFence()` | StoreStore + LoadStore | Medium |
| `VarHandle.loadLoadFence()` | LoadLoad only | Low |
| `VarHandle.storeStoreFence()` | StoreStore only | Low |

### When do you use fences versus volatile?

In application code, always use `volatile`, `synchronized`, or the atomic classes. They insert the correct fences automatically and express intent clearly. VarHandle fences are for lock-free data-structure authors who need minimal, precise ordering for maximum performance.

### How does volatile relate to barriers?

A `volatile` write inserts a StoreStore barrier before the write and a StoreLoad barrier after the write. A `volatile` read inserts a LoadLoad barrier after the read and a LoadStore barrier after the read. Together, these establish the happens-before relationship defined by the Java Memory Model.

```java
// volatile write is equivalent to:
StoreStore barrier;
write(field, value);
StoreLoad barrier;

// volatile read is equivalent to:
read(field);
LoadLoad barrier;
LoadStore barrier;
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using plain field writes across threads without any barrier or volatile keyword. The reader may see stale data or a reordered sequence.

```java
// WRONG: no barrier, consumer may see ready=true but stale amount
private BigDecimal amount;
private boolean ready;

public void publish() {
    this.amount = new BigDecimal("50000");
    this.ready = true;  // plain write, may be reordered before amount!
}

// CORRECT: volatile ensures ordering
private BigDecimal amount;
private volatile boolean ready;

public void publish() {
    this.amount = new BigDecimal("50000");
    this.ready = true;  // volatile write inserts StoreStore + StoreLoad
    // Consumer reading ready=true is guaranteed to see amount=50000
}
```

### Why is StoreLoad the most expensive barrier?

StoreLoad drains the entire store buffer, forcing all pending writes to become globally visible before any subsequent read. On x86, this is implemented as an `MFENCE` or `lock` prefix instruction. Other barriers are often free on x86 because the architecture already provides strong ordering (Total Store Order), but StoreLoad is always required.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the difference between a compiler barrier and a CPU barrier? A compiler barrier prevents the compiler from reordering. A CPU barrier prevents the hardware from reordering. Both are needed.
- How does the Java Memory Model relate to actual hardware barriers? The JMM defines happens-before relationships. The JVM emits the minimum hardware barriers needed to satisfy these relationships on each architecture.
- Why is x86 easier for concurrency than ARM? x86 provides Total Store Order (TSO), making most barriers free except StoreLoad. ARM has a weaker memory model, requiring more explicit barriers.
